Here is a detailed proof of concept:

```solidity
function test_transferPoints_DoSByFrontrun() external {
    AllocationVesting.AllocationSplit[] memory allocationSplits = new AllocationVesting.AllocationSplit[](2);
    // allocate to 2 users 50% 50%
    allocationSplits[0].recipient = users.user1; //Alice
    allocationSplits[0].points = INIT_USER_POINTS;
    allocationSplits[0].numberOfWeeks = 4;
    allocationSplits[1].recipient = users.user2; //Bob
    allocationSplits[1].points = INIT_USER_POINTS;
    allocationSplits[1].numberOfWeeks = 8;
    // setup allocations
    uint256 vestingStart = block.timestamp + 1 weeks;
    allocationVesting.setAllocations(allocationSplits, vestingStart);
    // warp to vesting finish time of user1
    vm.warp(vestingStart + 4 weeks);
    // user1 transfers points(but claimed all) to user3
    vm.prank(users.user1);
    allocationVesting.transferPoints(users.user1, users.user3, INIT_USER_POINTS);
    // user1 has claimed all allocated vesting tokens
    assertEq(allocationVesting.vestingToken().balanceOf(users.user1), totalAllocation / 2);
    // user2 can't transfer to user3
    vm.expectRevert(abi.encodeWithSelector(AllocationVesting.IncompatibleVestingPeriod.selector, 8, 4));
    vm.prank(users.user2);
    allocationVesting.transferPoints(users.user2, users.user3, INIT_USER_POINTS);
}
```

description:
According to the Cyfrin audit report 7.1.3, Bima team will maintain a Trove in each TroveManager with minimal debt and highest CR to make sure each Trove users create are liquidatable. We will handle this by having our own trove on each TroveManager with minimal debt and highest CR. This will ensure everyone is liquidatable and also during sunsetting we can just close our own trove which will be the final one.

The issue here is, when redeeming collateral, Bima allows users to redeem all troves, including the one Bima team is maintaining. If this happens, the above invariant will break, and the original issue "Impossible to liquidate borrower when a TroveManager instance only has 1 active borrower" will arise again. Note that in original LiquityV1, users are not allowed to redeem the last trove. This constraint is added in their closeTrove() function.
• TroveManager.sol#L1248:

```solidity
function _closeTrove(address _borrower, Status closedStatus) internal {
    assert(closedStatus != Status.nonExistent && closedStatus != Status.active);
    uint TroveOwnersArrayLength = TroveOwners.length;
    _requireMoreThanOneTroveInSystem(TroveOwnersArrayLength); // <<<
    Troves[_borrower].status = closedStatus;
    Troves[_borrower].coll = 0;
    Troves[_borrower].debt = 0;
    rewardSnapshots[_borrower].ETH = 0;
    rewardSnapshots[_borrower].LUSDDebt = 0;
    _removeTroveOwner(_borrower, TroveOwnersArrayLength);
    sortedTroves.remove(_borrower);
}
```

However, in Bima, no such constraint is added. So this would allow users to come and redeem all troves, and closing the last trove Bima team is maintaining. Also, we can find that there is a function _resetState() that handles the closing of the final trove. However, from the comments, it should only happen during collateral sunset, but such constraint is not added either. Anyone can come close the last trove at any time, as long as they have enough debtTokens to perform redemption.

```solidity
function redeemCollateral(
    uint256 _debtAmount,
    address _firstRedemptionHint,
    address _upperPartialRedemptionHint,
    address _lowerPartialRedemptionHint,
    uint256 _partialRedemptionHintNICR,
    uint256 _maxIterations,
    uint256 _maxFeePercentage
) external {
    // ...
    _resetState(); // <<<
}
```

/**
`LiquidationManager`. Many liquidation paths involve redistributing debt and must be closed by repaying the debt or via a redemption.
*/

```solidity
function _resetState() private {
    if (TroveOwners.length == 0) {
        activeInterestIndex = INTEREST_PRECISION;
        lastActiveIndexUpdate = block.timestamp;
        totalStakes = 0;
        totalStakesSnapshot = 0;
        totalCollateralSnapshot = 0;
        L_collateral = 0;
        L_debt = 0;
        lastCollateralError_Redistribution = 0;
        lastDebtError_Redistribution = 0;
        totalActiveCollateral = 0;
        totalActiveDebt = 0;
        defaultedCollateral = 0;
        defaultedDebt = 0;
    }
}
```

Impact: Users can freely redeem the collateral from all troves, including the one Bima team is maintaining. This breaks the invariant of Bima team maintaining a trove with minimum debt and largest CR, which is used to make all other troves liquidatable.

The ConvexDepositToken contract's CVX reward claiming mechanism is vulnerable on sidechains due to fundamental differences in Convex's reward architecture between mainnet and sidechains. Current implementation in ConvexDepositToken.sol:

```solidity
function _fetchRewards() internal {
    uint256 bimaAmount;
    uint256 id = emissionId;
    if (id > 0) bimaAmount = vault.allocateNewEmissions(id);
    crvRewards.getReward(address(this), false);
    cvxRewards.getReward();
    // @audit-poc side chain func sig is diff
}
```

From the docs you can see that the issue stems from three key issues: The first is function Signature mismatch, in mainnet: getReward() - simple claim → but in sidechains: getReward(address account, address forwardTo) - requires forwarding address. On sidechains, getReward() is unguarded and can be called by anyone. Without proper reward forwarding setup, this allows malicious actors to claim rewards meant for legitimate users. Third on sidechains provide a protection mechanism through setRewardRedirect(address _to) which our contract doesn't utilize, leaving rewards vulnerable to front-running. Impact: Malicious actors can front-run and steal CVX rewards on sidechain deployments due to unprotected reward claims.

poc:
The proof of concept demonstrates that on sidechains, unlike ETH mainnet, CVX rewards can be front-run & stolen cuz Convex's sidechain implementation has an unguarded getReward(account, forwardTo) function. The test proves this by showing an attacker can claim all rewards (100e18 tokens) first, leaving legitimate users with nothing when they try to claim ("Rewards already claimed" error):

```solidity
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;
import "forge-std/Test.sol";
import {IERC20} from "@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/IERC20.sol";

contract ConvexRewardExploitTest is Test {
    MockMainnetCVXRewards mainnetRewards;
    MockSidechainCVXRewards sidechainRewards;
    MockToken cvxToken;
    MockConvexDepositToken depositToken;
    address user = address(0x1);
    address attacker = address(0x2);

    function setUp() public {
        cvxToken = new MockToken("CVX", "CVX");
        mainnetRewards = new MockMainnetCVXRewards(cvxToken);
        sidechainRewards = new MockSidechainCVXRewards(cvxToken);
        // Mint initial rewards
        cvxToken.mint(address(mainnetRewards), 1000e18);
        cvxToken.mint(address(sidechainRewards), 100e18); // Only 100e18 total rewards
    }

    function testRewardClaimingMainnet() public {
        vm.chainId(1); // Set to mainnet
        // Setup mainnet deposit token
        depositToken = new MockConvexDepositToken(address(mainnetRewards), cvxToken);
        // User claims rewards - should work fine
        vm.prank(user);
        depositToken.fetchRewards();
        // Verify user got rewards
        assertGt(cvxToken.balanceOf(address(depositToken)), 0, "Should have received rewards on mainnet");
    }

    function testRewardClaimingArbitrum() public {
        vm.chainId(42161); // Set to Arbitrum
        depositToken = new MockConvexDepositToken(address(sidechainRewards), cvxToken);
        // Record initial balances
        uint256 initialAttackerBalance = cvxToken.balanceOf(attacker);
        // Attacker front-runs and claims all rewards
        vm.prank(attacker);
        sidechainRewards.getReward(attacker, attacker);
        // Verify attacker got all rewards
        assertEq(
            cvxToken.balanceOf(attacker) - initialAttackerBalance,
            100e18,
            "Attacker should get all rewards"
        );
        // User's claim should revert
        vm.expectRevert("Rewards already claimed");
        vm.prank(user);
        depositToken.fetchRewards();
    }
}

// Mock Token contract
contract MockToken is IERC20 {
    string public name;
    string public symbol;
    uint8 public constant decimals = 18;
    uint256 public totalSupply;
    mapping(address => uint256) public balanceOf;
    mapping(address => mapping(address => uint256)) public allowance;

    constructor(string memory _name, string memory _symbol) {
        name = _name;
        symbol = _symbol;
    }

    function mint(address to, uint256 amount) external {
        balanceOf[to] += amount;
        totalSupply += amount;
        emit Transfer(address(0), to, amount);
    }

    function transfer(address to, uint256 amount) external returns (bool) {
        balanceOf[msg.sender] -= amount;
        balanceOf[to] += amount;
        emit Transfer(msg.sender, to, amount);
        return true;
    }

    function transferFrom(address from, address to, uint256 amount) external returns (bool) {
        if (allowance[from][msg.sender] != type(uint256).max) {
            allowance[from][msg.sender] -= amount;
        }
        balanceOf[from] -= amount;
        balanceOf[to] += amount;
        emit Transfer(from, to, amount);
        return true;
    }

    function approve(address spender, uint256 amount) external returns (bool) {
        allowance[msg.sender][spender] = amount;
        emit Approval(msg.sender, spender, amount);
        return true;
    }
}

// Mock Mainnet CVX Rewards
contract MockMainnetCVXRewards {
    IERC20 public cvxToken;
    bool public rewardsClaimed;

    constructor(IERC20 _cvxToken) {
        cvxToken = _cvxToken;
    }

    function getReward() external {
        require(!rewardsClaimed, "Rewards already claimed");
        // Send fixed reward amount
        cvxToken.transfer(msg.sender, 100e18);
        rewardsClaimed = true;
    }
}

// Mock Sidechain CVX Rewards
contract MockSidechainCVXRewards {
    IERC20 public cvxToken;
    mapping(address => address) public rewardRedirect;
    bool public rewardsClaimed;

    constructor(IERC20 _cvxToken) {
        cvxToken = _cvxToken;
    }

    function getReward(address account, address forwardTo) external {
        require(!rewardsClaimed, "Rewards already claimed");
        address recipient = rewardRedirect[account] != address(0) ? rewardRedirect[account] : forwardTo;
        // Transfer all rewards and mark as claimed
        uint256 balance = cvxToken.balanceOf(address(this));
        cvxToken.transfer(recipient, balance);
        rewardsClaimed = true;
    }

    function setRewardRedirect(address _to) external {
        rewardRedirect[msg.sender] = _to;
    }
}

// Mock Convex Deposit Token (vulnerable version)
contract MockConvexDepositToken {
    address public cvxRewards;
    IERC20 public cvxToken;

    constructor(address _cvxRewards, IERC20 _cvxToken) {
        cvxRewards = _cvxRewards;
        cvxToken = _cvxToken;
    }

    function fetchRewards() external {
        // Vulnerable implementation that doesn't handle sidechains properly
        if (block.chainid == 1) {
            MockMainnetCVXRewards(cvxRewards).getReward();
        } else {
            // This will fail or be vulnerable on sidechains
            MockSidechainCVXRewards(cvxRewards).getReward(address(this), address(this));
        }
    }
}
```

description:
BaseRewardPool of Convex finance allows using mainly 2 different methods to get rewards one is:
```solidity
function getReward(address _account, bool _claimExtras) public
```
and other is:
```solidity
function getReward() external returns(bool)
```
as can be seen at #L980 - #L998 in the mainnet address 0x3Fe65692bfCD0e6CF84cB1E7d24108E434A7587e. The second one sets extra rewards to true by default. As it can be seen, the crv Reward pool sets extra rewards to false but cvx Rewards pool uses the second method here:
• ConvexDepositToken.sol#L284C1-L285C32:
```solidity
crvRewards.getReward(address(this), false);
cvxRewards.getReward(); //<@here
```
So the extra rewards are received by ConvexDepositToken.sol contract here. But the problem is it doesn't have a function to transfer out these extra rewards from the contract, similar to the one present in Curve Proxy as can be seen here:
• CurveProxy.sol#L255C3-L268C6:
```solidity
/**
@notice Transfer arbitrary token balances out of this contract
@dev Necessary for handling extra rewards on older gauge types
*/
function transferTokens(
address receiver,
TokenBalance[] calldata balances
) external onlyDepositManager returns (bool success) {
for (uint256 i; i < balances.length; i++) {
    balances[i].token.safeTransfer(receiver, balances[i].amount);
}
success = true;
}
```
Impact Explanation:
Extra tokens are withdrawn on behalf of ConvexDepositToken.sol, but there is no way to transfer them out causing them to be stuck on ConvexDepositToken.sol contract forever.

