            // expected value would be 4e18
            require(token.totalWeight() == 5e18);
        }

As we can see, we can call `token.incrementGauge(gauge2, 1e18)` before `token.addGauge(gauge2)`. This is because [this check](https://github.com/fei-protocol/flywheel-v2/blob/77bfadf388db25cf5917d39cd9c0ad920f404aad/src/token/ERC20Gauges.sol#L257) doesn’t revert for gauges that were never added in the first place.

First time the total weight is incremented in `_incrementUserAndGlobalWeights` and 2nd time [here](https://github.com/fei-protocol/flywheel-v2/blob/77bfadf388db25cf5917d39cd9c0ad920f404aad/src/token/ERC20Gauges.sol#L465-L469).

If corrupting state like this is adventurous for someone, he can frontrun `token.addGauge` called by the admin with a call to `incrementGauge` which is permissionless.

description:
A pool can be initialized with a splits in the range [12; 19]. However, a pool initialized with a 19 splits will never be usable. This is due to an issue in the JumpyFallback library, it tries to read at an out-of-bounds index in an array.
The JumpyFallback.Info structure initializes the tree field with 1 << MAX_SPLITS elements. This corresponds to 1 << 19 = 524288. The first element will be tree[0] and the last one tree[524287].
However, the JumpyFallback.at function calculates a cursor that starts at [1 << splits]. When splits = 19, it starts at 524288:
```solidity
int256 cursor = ((1 << splits.toUint256()).toInt256() + offset);
while (!(self.tree[cursor.toUint256()].deadlineJumps.touched)) {
    if (((cursor >> MIN_SPLITS) == 1)) return POSITIVE_ZERO;
    cursor = (cursor >> 1);
}
```
Then, reading at self.tree[524288] will revert with the error panic: array out-of-bounds access as this index does not exist in the array.

