description:
In the case of an incoming transaction, funds are pulled from the counterparty.
```solidity
if (_incoming) {
    // ...
    IERC20(_tokenContract).transferFrom(_counterparty, address(this), _amount);
}
```
Hence, when the timelock expires and the counterparty requests a refund for this incoming transaction, funds should be released back. However, the implementation logic is inverted, where funds are instead released for outgoing transactions.

When bridging successfully from the Lightning network to an EVM chain, a two steps process is followed:
• Step 1: TokenForwardHTLC logic is executed. It approves the ERC20 token amount to the HTLC contract before calling the route function on this same HTLC contract.
• Step 2: TokenSettleHTLC logic is executed. The settle function is called on the HTLC contract to transfer funds to the receiver (i.e. the counterparty).
However, the TokenForwardHTLC contract is not supposed to take funds from the plugin wallet. The ERC20 approval isn't expected.
```solidity
function route(
address _counterparty,
bool _incoming,
bytes32 _hashlock,
uint256 _timelock,
address _tokenContract,
uint256 _amount
) external transferable returns (bool) {
    require(_tokenContract == tokenContract, "Token is not allowed");
    require(_counterparty != address(0), "Counterparty can't be zero address");
    require(_amount > 0, "Token amount must be > 0");
    require(_hashlock != bytes32(0), "Hashlock can't be zero");
    require(_timelock > block.timestamp, "Timelock time must be in the future");
    require(_timelock < block.timestamp + 1209600, "Timelock time must be not too far in the future");
    if (_incoming) {
        // leaving redundant check for better safety
        require(IERC20(_tokenContract).allowance(_counterparty, address(this)) == _amount, "Token allowance must be equal to amount");
        IERC20(_tokenContract).safeTransferFrom(_counterparty, address(this), _amount);
    } else {
        require(msg.sender == owner(), "");
    }
    incoming = _incoming;
    counterparty = _counterparty;
    amount = _amount;
    timelock = _timelock;
    hashlock = _hashlock;
    emit HTLCERC20New(_incoming, _counterparty, _tokenContract, _amount, _hashlock, _timelock);
    return true;
}
```
Then, this approval allows an external party to create route with counterparty being the plugin wallet to pull funds from it.

