In the SatoshiSwap protocol, there is a privileged owner account that plays a critical role in governing and regulating the system-wide operations (e.g., pool addition, reward adjustment, and parameter setting). It also has the privilege to control or govern the flow of assets managed by this protocol. In the following, we examine the privileged account and their related privileged accesses in current contracts.
```solidity
// Set the migrator contract. Can only be called by the owner.
function setMigrator(IMigratorChef _migrator) public onlyOwner {
    migrator = _migrator;
}
```
```solidity
// Public Migrate lp token to another contract. Can be called by anyone. We trust that migrator contract is good.
function migrate(uint256 _pid) public {
    require(address(migrator) != address(0), "migrate: no migrator");
    PoolInfo storage pool = poolInfo[_pid];
    IERC20 lpToken = pool.lpToken;
    uint256 bal = lpToken.balanceOf(address(this));
    lpToken.safeApprove(address(migrator), bal);
    IERC20 newLpToken = migrator.migrate(lpToken);
    require(bal == newLpToken.balanceOf(address(this)), "migrate: bad");
    pool.lpToken = newLpToken;
}
```
Specifically, the MasterChef contract supports a migration feature that can migrate current pool liquidity to another contract. Notice that the privilege assignment may be necessary and consistent with the protocol design. In the meantime, the extra power to the owner may also be a counter-party risk to the token users. Therefore, we list this concern as an issue here from the audit perspective and highly recommend making these onlyOwner privileges explicit or raising necessary awareness among protocol users. Moreover, the management account could withdraw all protectedTokens, which are want tokens, from the pool in emergency.
```solidity
// emergency withdraw
function emergencyWithdraw() external management {
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < protectedTokens().length; i++) {
        IERC20Upgradeable(protectedTokens()[i]).safeTransfer(
            IVault(strategy).governance(),
            IERC20Upgradeable(protectedTokens()[i]).balanceOf(address(this))
        );
    }
    IPositionStorage(positionStorage).emergencyWithdraw();
}
```
It is worrisome if the privileged owner account is a plain EOA account. Note that a multi-sig account could greatly alleviate this concern, though it is still far from perfect. Specifically, a better approach is to eliminate the administration key concern by transferring the role to a community-governed DAO.

