Malicious order keepers can trigger the cancellation of any order by providing oracle prices from blocks unrelated to the order. Each order has its own requirements about what block ranges it expects. The error handler for each order only allows orders to be retried if the keeper provided empty/invalid prices. The error handlers make no such allowances for invalid blocks. The order keepers are supposed to be a decentralized network, so it's likely that at some point there will be a bad actor, or one who uses the rules to their advantage, at the expense of other users. Orders will be canceled rather than retried by keepers that would have executed the order properly. This may lead to position liquidations.

